Chapter: CpuInspector
  cites Dev chapter HexFormat
  cites Foreword chapter CCE

 What the processor says about itself: the two control registers this
 machine actually depends on, a raw CPUID leaf, and the feature bits
 decoded into names.

 THE WHOLE CHAPTER IS PURE, and that is a statement about the builtins
 rather than about the code. `cpu-read-cr0`, `cpu-read-cr3` and the four
 `cpu-cpuid-*` carry no effect row: they read processor state and change
 nothing, so reading them is an ordinary function call and every report
 below can be built during rendering rather than cached. That is the
 opposite of the disk inspector, where every read costs 512 bytes of heap
 that is never returned.

 A CPUID CALL IS NOT FREE THOUGH, and the difference matters once. CPUID
 is a serializing instruction: it drains the pipeline, so it is orders of
 magnitude more expensive than the `peek` behind every other inspector
 here. Four calls per leaf, and the feature report reads three leaves, so
 `cpu.feat` is a dozen serializing instructions. That is nothing on a
 command driven by a keypress and would be ruinous in a loop, which is why
 nothing here loops over leaves.

 THE FEATURE TABLE NAMES ONLY BITS WORTH NAMING. A full decode of every
 defined CPUID bit is hundreds of rows that go stale as vendors add more,
 and an inspector that renamed or mis-numbered one would be worse than no
 inspector. What is here is the set this project's own code depends on or
 asks about: long mode and NX because the boot path requires them, the SSE
 family because the vector types lower to it, RDRAND because the identity
 code wants entropy, and HYPERVISOR because it answers "am I under
 codex-vm" in one bit.

 THE VENDOR STRING CROSSES THE CCE BOUNDARY IN THE INBOUND DIRECTION.
 CPUID returns twelve ASCII bytes packed across three registers, and Codex
 Text is CCE, so a byte appended directly would build a Text whose
 characters are numerically ASCII. `from-unicode` is what converts, and it
 answers negative one for a byte it cannot map, which is refused rather
 than appended. This is the same trap that put a TLS hostname on the wire
 in CCE until 2026-07-13, running the other way.

 We say:

Section: Control Register Zero

 The bits that decide what mode the processor is in. PE and PG are the two
 that MUST be set on this machine: long mode requires paging, and paging
 requires protected mode, so a zero in either is not a configuration
 choice but a machine that should not be executing this code.

  ci-cr0-line : Text
  ci-cr0-line =
    let v = cpu-read-cr0
    in "CR0 #" & u32-to-hex v & "  " & ci-cr0-flags v

  ci-cr0-flags : Integer -> Text
  ci-cr0-flags (v) =
    let a = "PE=" & bit-flag v 0 & " MP=" & bit-flag v 1 & " EM=" & bit-flag v 2
    in let b = " TS=" & bit-flag v 3 & " NE=" & bit-flag v 5 & " WP=" & bit-flag v 16
    in a & b & " AM=" & bit-flag v 18 & " NW=" & bit-flag v 29 & " CD=" & bit-flag v 30 & " PG=" & bit-flag v 31

Section: Control Register Three

 CR3 is the physical address of the top-level page table plus two cache
 bits in its low half. The address is reported separately from the raw
 value because the raw value is not an address: the low twelve bits are
 flags, and an operator who took CR3 as a pointer would be off by whatever
 those bits happen to be.

  ci-cr3-line : Text
  ci-cr3-line =
    let v = cpu-read-cr3
    in "CR3 #" & addr-to-hex v & "  PML4 #" & addr-to-hex (bit-and v (bit-not 4095)) & "  PWT=" & bit-flag v 3 & " PCD=" & bit-flag v 4

  ci-cr-report : List Text
  ci-cr-report = [ci-cr0-line, ci-cr3-line]

Section: One Leaf

 The four registers of one leaf, raw. This is the command that answers a
 question the decode below cannot, because the decode only knows the bits
 it was taught: a leaf nobody has named still shows its four words here.

 Every leaf is read with subleaf zero, which the builtins enforce by
 zeroing ECX. A leaf whose meaning depends on the subleaf therefore shows
 only its first, and that is stated rather than left to surprise someone
 reading leaf 4 or leaf 0x0D.

  ci-leaf-line : Integer -> Text
  ci-leaf-line (leaf) =
    let a = "eax #" & u32-to-hex (cpu-cpuid-eax leaf) & "  ebx #" & u32-to-hex (cpu-cpuid-ebx leaf)
    in "leaf #" & u32-to-hex leaf & "  " & a & "  ecx #" & u32-to-hex (cpu-cpuid-ecx leaf) & "  edx #" & u32-to-hex (cpu-cpuid-edx leaf)

 The maximum leaf comes from leaf zero, and asking beyond it is not an
 error the processor reports: it answers the highest leaf it does know, or
 zeroes, with nothing to distinguish that from a real answer. So the
 refusal is here rather than left to the reader.

  ci-max-leaf : Integer
  ci-max-leaf = cpu-cpuid-eax 0

  ci-leaf-report : Integer -> List Text
  ci-leaf-report (leaf) =
    if leaf < 0 then ["refused: leaf " & show leaf & " is not a leaf"]
    else if leaf > ci-max-leaf then
      ["refused: leaf #" & u32-to-hex leaf & " is above the maximum this processor reports (#" & u32-to-hex ci-max-leaf & ")",
       "extended leaves start at #80000000 and are not covered by that maximum"]
    else [ci-leaf-line leaf]

Section: The Vendor

  ci-char-for : Integer -> Text
  ci-char-for (b) =
    let cce = from-unicode b
    in if cce < 0 then "" else char-to-text (code-to-char cce)

  ci-word-text : Integer -> Text
  ci-word-text (w) =
    let a = ci-char-for (bit-and w 255) & ci-char-for (bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255)
    in a & ci-char-for (bit-and (bit-shru w 16) 255) & ci-char-for (bit-and (bit-shru w 24) 255)

 EBX then EDX then ECX, which is not a typo and not alphabetical: it is
 the order the twelve bytes are defined in. Reading them in register order
 spells `GenuntelineI` on an Intel part, which looks enough like a working
 decode to be believed.

  ci-vendor : Text
  ci-vendor = ci-word-text (cpu-cpuid-ebx 0) & ci-word-text (cpu-cpuid-edx 0) & ci-word-text (cpu-cpuid-ecx 0)

Section: Features

  ci-feat-name : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  ci-feat-name (v) (bit) (nm) = if bit-and v (bit-shl 1 bit) /= 0 then nm else ""

  ci-join : Text, Text -> Text
  ci-join (acc) (nm) =
    if text-length nm == 0 then acc
    else if text-length acc == 0 then nm
    else acc & " " & nm

 A word for an empty set, because an empty feature line reads as a decode
 that failed rather than as a register that is genuinely zero. This is not
 hypothetical: leaf 1 ECX reads zero under codex-vm, so the line for it is
 empty on the machine this is most often run on, and a reader would
 reasonably suspect the inspector before suspecting the host.

  ci-or-none : Text -> Text
  ci-or-none (t) = if text-length t == 0 then "(none)" else t

 Leaf 1 EDX is the original feature word and holds the things the compiler
 assumes rather than checks: SSE2 is where the vector types land, and TSC
 is what the profiler reads.

  ci-leaf1-edx : Text
  ci-leaf1-edx =
    let v = cpu-cpuid-edx 1
    in let a = ci-join (ci-join "" (ci-feat-name v 0 "FPU")) (ci-feat-name v 4 "TSC")
    in let b = ci-join (ci-join a (ci-feat-name v 5 "MSR")) (ci-feat-name v 6 "PAE")
    in let c = ci-join (ci-join b (ci-feat-name v 9 "APIC")) (ci-feat-name v 15 "CMOV")
    in let d = ci-join (ci-join c (ci-feat-name v 23 "MMX")) (ci-feat-name v 24 "FXSR")
    in ci-join (ci-join d (ci-feat-name v 25 "SSE")) (ci-feat-name v 26 "SSE2")

 Leaf 1 ECX is the later word. HYPERVISOR is bit 31 and is the one bit
 here that answers a question about the ENVIRONMENT rather than the
 processor: it is set under codex-vm and clear on metal, so it separates
 the two cases a bare-metal debugger most needs to tell apart.

  ci-leaf1-ecx : Text
  ci-leaf1-ecx =
    let v = cpu-cpuid-ecx 1
    in let a = ci-join (ci-join "" (ci-feat-name v 0 "SSE3")) (ci-feat-name v 9 "SSSE3")
    in let b = ci-join (ci-join a (ci-feat-name v 19 "SSE4.1")) (ci-feat-name v 20 "SSE4.2")
    in let c = ci-join (ci-join b (ci-feat-name v 25 "AES")) (ci-feat-name v 28 "AVX")
    in ci-join (ci-join c (ci-feat-name v 30 "RDRAND")) (ci-feat-name v 31 "HYPERVISOR")

 The extended leaf, and the two bits the boot path depends on: LM is long
 mode itself, and NX is the no-execute page bit. A processor answering
 zero for LM cannot be running this code, so a blank here means the leaf
 was not read rather than that the feature is missing.

  ci-ext-edx : Text
  ci-ext-edx =
    let v = cpu-cpuid-edx 2147483649
    in let a = ci-join (ci-join "" (ci-feat-name v 20 "NX")) (ci-feat-name v 26 "1GB-PAGES")
    in ci-join a (ci-feat-name v 29 "LONG-MODE")

  ci-feat-report : List Text
  ci-feat-report =
    ["vendor " & ci-vendor & "  max leaf #" & u32-to-hex ci-max-leaf,
     "leaf1.edx " & ci-or-none ci-leaf1-edx,
     "leaf1.ecx " & ci-or-none ci-leaf1-ecx,
     "ext.edx   " & ci-or-none ci-ext-edx]
